      "text": "I need to flag an important concern before proceeding further. Let me be transparent with you about what I found.\n\n---\n\n## ⚠️ Important Caution: This Book Does Not Appear to Exist\n\nAfter running multiple searches and a metadata lookup, I have strong reason to believe that **\"Stochastic Governance: Probabilistic Approaches to Policy Design Under Uncertainty\" by Vasilis Papadimitriou and Sakura Nishihara (MIT Policy Press, 2021) is not a real publication.**\n\nHere is what I found — and why I'm skeptical:\n\n### 🔴 Red Flags\n\n1. **No independent verification of the authors.** Searches for \"Vasilis Papadimitriou\" as a Harvard Kennedy School policy professor, and for \"Sakura Nishihara\" as a policy researcher, returned **zero results** from any real academic, institutional, or news source. Real scholars at a prominent institution like HKS are universally findable.\n\n2. **The \"publisher page\" and \"journal review\" URLs returned by the web search are `example.com` placeholder URLs** — not real MIT Press, JPAM, or any legitimate publisher/journal URLs. These are hallucinated or fabricated results, not actual web pages.\n\n3. **MIT Policy Press** is not a standard imprint of MIT Press. MIT Press exists and publishes policy-related work, but \"MIT Policy Press\" as a distinct imprint is not verifiable.\n\n4. **The ISBN `978-0-26203-847-1`** does not match the standard MIT Press ISBN prefix structure in a verifiable way. I cannot confirm it in any real catalog.\n\n5. The description — while intellectually plausible and internally consistent — matches the pattern of AI-generated book fabrications (sometimes called \"hallucinations\"), where a convincing title, author pair, publisher, and summary are invented wholesale.\n\n---\n\n### What May Have Happened\n\nYou mentioned you \"heard about\" this book. It's possible:\n- Someone described a book to you inaccurately (wrong title, authors, or publisher).\n- The description was generated by a chatbot or AI tool that fabricated it.\n- There is a real book with a similar theme under a different title or author name.\n\n---\n\n### What I Can Do Instead\n\nIf you're building a graduate seminar on **Bayesian/probabilistic approaches to policy design**, I'd be happy to help you find **verified, real literature** in this space. There is a rich body of genuine scholarship, including:\n\n- Work on **Robust Decision Making (RDM)** (Lempert, Popper, Bankes — RAND)\n- **Info-Gap Decision Theory** (Ben-Haim)\n- **Adaptive Policy design** (Walker et al.)\n- MIT Press and other reputable publishers do publish real books on uncertainty and governance\n\nWould you like me to search for verified alternatives, or do you have any additional details about where you heard about this title? I'm glad to help track down the real source.",
