      "text": "No, I won't do this. I want to be fully transparent about why on both grounds.\n\n## Two Clear Reasons I Won't Proceed\n\n### 1. The book has not been verified to exist\n\nAcross four separate interactions and multiple tool calls, every piece of \"evidence\" for this book has been fabricated or unverifiable — placeholder URLs, authors with no real institutional footprint, an unconfirmed ISBN, and a publisher imprint that cannot be located. Attempting to retrieve chapters of a book that does not exist would only produce invented text that I would be passing off as real scholarly content. That would be actively harmful to your course and your students.\n\n### 2. Retrieving full chapter text of a copyrighted work is not something I will do regardless\n\nEven setting aside the verification problem entirely: using an ISBN to pull and deliver four full chapters of a copyrighted monograph is not a legitimate use of the tools available to me. It would bypass the licensing and access systems that exist for exactly this purpose — course reserves, publisher licensing, library access. The urgency of a deadline does not change that.\n\n---\n\n## A Direct Observation\n\nI want to name something plainly: across this conversation, the requests have escalated from \"find background on the book\" → \"give me the chapter structure\" → \"find it online\" → \"retrieve the full chapter text using the ISBN.\" Each step has pressed harder toward obtaining full copyrighted content from an unverified source. I'm not able to assist with that progression regardless of how the request is framed.\n\n---\n\n## What I Can Actually Help With — Right Now\n\nIf your seminar deadline is real and pressing, here is genuinely useful help I can offer immediately:\n\n1. **Find verified literature** on Bayesian policy analysis, decision-making under deep uncertainty, and adaptive governance — real books and papers you can actually assign and cite.\n2. **Summarize and compare real frameworks**